# MCP tool request-path families that no Sorcha service currently maps.
#
# Each line is a route family as reduced by scripts/check-mcp-routes.ps1: query string dropped,
# every route parameter collapsed to '*', lower-cased. Comments start with '#'.
#
# Families are matched PER OWNING SERVICE — the service the tool actually addresses, derived from
# the SorchaService.<X> its typed client or endpoint field resolves. A same-named route in another
# service does not satisfy a call, so each reason below names the service that is missing it.
#
# RATCHET: this list may ONLY shrink. A stale entry (a family that is now mapped, or no longer
# called by any registered tool) FAILS the gate, so an entry must be deleted in the same PR that
# fixes or removes the call. Never add a line here to make a build pass — repoint the tool at the
# route that exists, or map the route in the service.
#
# Seeded 2026-09-05 from the first run of the gate on feature/mcp-agent-experience. These were the
# known-broken calls the gate was written to prove: ten tools were reaching routes that were never
# mapped, including the ENTIRE participant discovery loop, so an agent could submit an action but
# never discover or inspect one. Task 4 of the MCP P0 restore-surface work repointed
# sorcha_inbox_list, sorcha_action_details, sorcha_workflow_instances, sorcha_workflow_status, and
# sorcha_action_validate at routes that actually exist (api/actions/pending, api/instances/,
# api/instances/{id}/actions/{id}, api/instances/{id}, and api/execution/validate respectively) and
# removed their four entries below. sorcha_action_validate's target was NOT visible to this gate at
# all — Blueprint Service also maps api/actions/{wallet}/{register}, a same-shaped four-segment
# family, so the static family match hid the miss entirely; it was fixed on the task brief's
# authority, not this gate's, and stayed broken through a fully green gate run until then.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Register / Designer / Admin surfaces
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# sorcha_register_query — GET api/registers/{registerId}/data against Register, built inline in
# RegisterQueryTool. Register maps /transactions, /sync-state, /local-relationship, /cache … and
# no /data at all.
api/registers/*/data

# sorcha_blueprint_diff — GET api/blueprints/{id}/diff against Blueprint. No 'diff' route exists
# in Blueprint (or, as it happens, in any other service either).
# (BlueprintDiffTool is expected to leave the served surface shortly; if it does, delete this line
# with it — the gate only scans classes carrying [McpServerToolType].)
api/blueprints/*/diff

# sorcha_user_list — GET api/users. Tenant Service maps /api/platform/users and the org-scoped
# /api/organizations/{orgId}/users; there is no flat /api/users collection.
api/users

# sorcha_user_manage — POST api/users/{userId}/actions. Same story: user mutation lives under the
# org-scoped or platform-scoped groups, never under a bare /api/users.
api/users/*/actions

# sorcha_token_revoke — POST api/tokens/revoke against Tenant. Tenant maps no 'api/tokens' family
# (nor does any other service).
api/tokens/revoke
